+**IV. THE FIRST WEAK-COUPLING GAP, A THEOREM FOR THE FIRST NIGHT.** The four-dimensional Z₂ gauge theory is self-dual (Wegner 1971: tanh β* = e^{−2β}, the self-dual point β_c = ½ ln(1 + √2) = 0.4407); the duality is MacWilliams on the plaquette code — THIS TREE'S THEOREM Α2 — and the plaquette-energy correlations are self-dual observables. Therefore Dobrushin's uniform gap at β* < 0.128 (Β3 with the concurrent seat's per-site lemma) IS a uniform gap at β > −½ ln tanh(0.128) = 1.03: THE MASS GAP OF THE 4D Z₂ GAUGE THEORY AT WEAK COUPLING, by this tree's own duality theorem composed with its own Dobrushin theorem — the first weak-coupling gap in the tree, both phases gapped, the gap closing only at the self-dual point, unreachable by either bound as it must be. For a FINITE group this is the whole story (the lattice gap grows with β: today's freezing readings; no continuum). For SU(N) there is no self-duality and no transition: the strong-coupling class extends to every β in the infrared — which is exactly the content of the crossover theorem.
